.if n .ad l
.nh
.SH "نام (NAME)"
geocpset \- adds control points based on geometric position
.SH "خلاصه دستور (SYNOPSIS)"
.IX Header "SYNOPSIS"
\&\fBgeocpset\fR [\fI\-o output.pto\fR] \fIinput.pto\fR
.SH "توضیحات (DESCRIPTION)"
.IX Header "DESCRIPTION"
\&\fBgeocpset\fR is a tool for panoramas which contains featureless images, e.g.
sky images. In this images a control point detector does not find control
points. \fBgeocpset\fR analyses the positions of the images in project files.
Then it adds control points to these images which are not connected by
control points. The control points are set only be the geometric positions
of the images and not by the image content. So \fBgeocpset\fR should only
used with projects which have assigned rough image positions.
.SH "نحوه استفاده (USAGE)"
.IX Header "USAGE"
.IP " \fBgeocpset\fR \fI\-o output.pto\fR \fIinput.pto\fR" 4
.IX Item " geocpset -o output.pto input.pto"
.PP
If the \fI\-\-output/\-o\fR switch is missing then the suffix "_geo" is added to
the filename.
.PP
The indented workflow is
.IP "#1 First create project file" 4
.IX Item "#1 First create project file"
pto_gen \-o project.pto *.jpg
.IP "#2 Then assign rough positions to images" 4
.IX Item "#2 Then assign rough positions to images"
pto_var \-\-set y=i*20\-40,p=0,r=0 \-o project.pto project.pto
.IP "#3 Now run a ""normal"" control point detector, e.g. only on overlapping images" 4
.IX Item "#3 Now run a ""normal"" control point detector, e.g. only on overlapping images"
cpfind \-\-prealigned \-o project.pto project.pto
.IP "#4 And finally connect all unconnected images" 4
.IX Item "#4 And finally connect all unconnected images"
geocpset \-o project.pto project.pto
.SH "گزینه‌ها (OPTIONS)"
.IX Header "OPTIONS"
.IP "\fB\-o|\-\-output\fR \fIoutput.pto\fR" 4
.IX Item "-o|--output output.pto"
Output a pto file with the given filename. If not given it will append
"_geo" to the input filename.
.IP \fB\-e|\-\-each\-overlap\fR 4
.IX Item "-e|--each-overlap"
By default \fBgeocpset\fR adds only control points to unconnected images.
With this switch you can force to add a geometric control point to each
overlap, which is not connect by control points or linked with image
positions.
.IP \fB\-\-min\-overlap=NUM\fR 4
.IX Item "--min-overlap=NUM"
By default \fBgeocpset\fR takes only image pairs into account which overlap
more than 10 %. If you have very narrow overlaps you can decrease this
value to take also such small overlaps into account, e.g.
\&\fB\-\-min\-overlap=1\fR
.IP \fB\-h|\-\-help\fR 4
.IX Item "-h|--help"
Display help.
.SH "نویسندگان (AUTHORS)"
.IX Header "AUTHORS"
\&\fBgeocpset\fR was written by Thomas Modes. This manpages was generated by
converting the wiki\-page on <http://wiki.panotools.org> to pod\-format by
Andreas Metzler.
